 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "cardiff_tiger_bay",
   "title": "Tiger Bay",
   "text": "Butetown, the docklands everyone called Tiger Bay, grew up round the busiest coal port in the world. From the late 1800s sailors from Somalia, Yemen, the Caribbean, West Africa, Norway and dozens of other places settled here, making it one of the oldest multi-ethnic communities in Britain and home to one of its oldest Muslim communities.",
   "people": "tiger_bay"
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "cardiff_shirley_bassey",
   "title": "Shirley Bassey",
   "text": "Dame Shirley Bassey was born in Tiger Bay in 1937, the youngest of seven children of a Nigerian seaman and an English mother. She sang in local pubs and clubs as a teenager and went on to sing three James Bond themes, Goldfinger among them. Butetown still claims her, loudly.",
   "people": "tiger_bay"
  },
  {
   "text": "THE VALLEYS. North: the Rhondda, then Merthyr Tydfil and Blaenavon. Coal, iron, chapels, choirs, and rain that goes sideways. Croeso!",
   "x": 44.0,
   "y": 2.0
  },
  {
   "local": {"cy": "Castell Caerdydd"},
   "text": "CARDIFF CASTLE. Roman fort, then Norman keep, then Victorian fantasy. The Marquess of Bute spent a fortune on towers.",
   "x": 28.0,
   "y": 15.0
  },
  {
   "local": {"cy": "Parc Bute"},
   "text": "BUTE PARK. 56 hectares, the River Taff, and a lot of cricket. Please do not feed the gulls. They have learnt to ask.",
   "x": 53.0,
   "y": 12.0
  },
  {
   "text": "CARDIFF ARENA. Home of rugby, concerts and the roof that rains inside. Entry through the big door. Dry feet not guaranteed.",
   "x": 22.0,
   "y": 31.0
  },
  {
   "local": {"cy": "Canolfan Mileniwm Cymru"},
   "text": "WALES MILLENNIUM CENTRE. The poem on the front reads, in Welsh: 'Creu gwir fel gwydr o ffwrnais awen'. Creating truth like glass from the furnace of inspiration.",
   "x": 35.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "local": {"cy": "Bae Caerdydd"},
   "text": "CARDIFF BAY. Once the biggest coal port in the world. Now, the biggest puddle with a view.",
   "x": 29.0,
   "y": 32.0
  },
  {
   "local": {"cy": "Adeilad y Pierhead"},
   "text": "PIERHEAD BUILDING. 1897, red terracotta. The Senedd is next door. They're very polite to each other.",
   "x": 23.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "text": "NORWEGIAN CHURCH. Roald Dahl was christened here. He didn't write about the font, but he did write about the giants.",
   "x": 50.0,
   "y": 32.0
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Roald Dahl, author, was born in Llandaff in 1916 and baptised in the Norwegian Church.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "cardiff_roald_dahl",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Roald Dahl"
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "cardiff_name",
   "title": "Name: Caerdydd",
   "text": "Cardiff's Welsh name is Caerdydd. Caer is Welsh for a fort, which fits, since the Romans built one where the castle now stands. The second half is usually linked to the River Taff, though scholars still argue over it, as scholars do."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "cardiff_coal_exchange",
   "title": "The Coal Exchange",
   "text": "In the Coal Exchange in Butetown, traders once agreed coal prices for much of the world. The story goes that the first cheque for a million pounds was signed there in 1904. The coal came down from the valleys by rail; the cheque just needed a lot of noughts."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "cardiff_terra_nova",
   "title": "Scott's Terra Nova",
   "text": "In June 1910 Captain Scott's ship Terra Nova sailed from Cardiff for the Antarctic, stocked with coal and money given by the city. Scott reached the South Pole in 1912, but his polar party never came home. A memorial lighthouse in Roath Park still remembers them."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "cardiff_glamorgan_sausage",
   "title": "Glamorgan Sausage",
   "text": "Selsig Morgannwg, the Glamorgan sausage, contains no meat at all: it is cheese, leeks and breadcrumbs rolled into a sausage and fried. Caerphilly is the traditional cheese. The Victorian travel writer George Borrow praised them, and vegetarians have been smug ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "arts_hen_wlad",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau",
   "text": "'Land of my fathers': the words by Evan James and the tune by his son James James, written in Pontypridd in January 1856. On 16 December 1905, when the All Blacks performed the haka at Cardiff Arms Park, the Welsh team and the crowd answered with the anthem, the first time one was sung before an international match anywhere. Wales won 3-0. It has no official status and needs none; seventy thousand people singing 'Gwlad! Gwlad!' is a law of its own."
  }
